1917, the \",[\"$\",\"$L40\",\"1\",{\"target\":\"_blank\",\"rel\":\"noopener noreferrer\",\"children\":[\"92nd and 93rd Divisions\"],\"data-sentry-element\":\"NextLink\",\"data-sentry-component\":\"Link\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"link.tsx\",\"href\":\"https://unwritten-record.blogs.archives.gov/2017/02/13/a-brief-look-at-african-american-soldiers-in-the-great-war/\",\"className\":\"mpgbmc0 _1jnz0oc1y g1fwxfm3 mpgbmc2 g1fwxflr\",\"prefetch\":false}],\". Trained separately and inadequately in the United States, the divisions fared differently in the war. The 92nd faced criticism for their performance in the Meuse-Argonne campaign in September 1918. The 93rd Division, however, had more success.\"]}],\"data-sentry-component\":\"Box\"}],[\"$\",\"div\",\"2\",{\"className\":\"_42elsx1 o2jrjz3 g1fwxfzs g1fwxfzw\",\"data-sentry-element\":\"Component\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"box.tsx\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"With dwindling armies, France asked America for reinforcements, and General \",[\"$\",\"$L40\",\"1\",{\"target\":\"_blank\",\"rel\":\"noopener noreferrer\",\"children\":[\"John Pershing\"],\"data-sentry-element\":\"NextLink\",\"data-sentry-component\":\"Link\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"link.tsx\",\"href\":\"/articles/world-war-i/john-j-pershing\",\"className\":\"mpgbmc0 _1jnz0oc1y g1fwxfm3 mpgbmc2 g1fwxflr\",\"prefetch\":false}],\", commander of the American Expeditionary Forces, sent regiments in the 93 Division to over, since France had experience fighting alongside Black soldiers from their Senegalese French Colonial army. The 93 Division’s 369 regiment, nicknamed the \",[\"$\",\"$L40\",\"3\",{\"target\":\"_blank\",\"rel\":\"noopener noreferrer\",\"children\":[\"Harlem Hellfighters\"],\"data-sentry-element\":\"NextLink\",\"data-sentry-component\":\"Link\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"link.tsx\",\"href\":\"/articles/world-war-i/the-harlem-hellfighters-video\",\"className\":\"mpgbmc0 _1jnz0oc1y g1fwxfm3 mpgbmc2 g1fwxflr\",\"prefet