ata-sentry-source-file\":\"box.tsx\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"In the years before World War I, the superiority of Britain’s Royal Navy was unchallenged by any other nation’s fleet, but the Imperial German Navy had made substantial strides in closing the gap between the two naval powers. Germany’s strength on the high seas was also aided by its lethal fleet of U-boat submarines.\"]}],\"data-sentry-component\":\"Box\"}]\ncb:[\"$\",\"div\",\"9\",{\"className\":\"_42elsx1 o2jrjz3 g1fwxfzs g1fwxfzw\",\"data-sentry-element\":\"Component\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"box.tsx\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"After the Battle of Dogger Bank in January 1915, in which the British mounted a "])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"surprise attack on German ships in the North Sea, the German navy chose not to confront Britain’s mighty Royal Navy in a major battle for more than a year, preferring to rest the bulk of its naval strategy on its U-boats.\"]}],\"data-sentry-component\":\"Box\"}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"cc:[\"$\",\"div\",\"10\",{\"className\":\"_42elsx1 o2jrjz3 g1fwxfzs g1fwxfzw\",\"data-sentry-element\":\"Component\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"box.tsx\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"The biggest naval engagement of World War I, the \",[\"$\",\"$L40\",\"1\",{\"target\":\"_blank\",\"rel\":\"noopener noreferrer\",\"children\":[\"Battle of Jutland\"],\"data-sentry-element\":\"NextLink\",\"data-sentry-component\":\"Link\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"link.tsx\",\"href\":\"/articles/world-war-i/battle-of-jutland\",\"className\":\"mpgbmc0 _1jnz0oc1y g1fwxfm3 mpgbmc2 g1fwxflr\",\"prefetch\":false}],\" (May 1916) left British naval superiority on the North Sea intact, and Germany would make no further attempts to break an Allied naval blockade for the remainder of the war.\"]}],\"data-sentry-component\":\"Box\"}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"cd:[\"$\",\"h2\",\"11\",{\"className\":\"_15il2ffd _15il2ffa a0xfey1 g1fwxfbr g1fwxfc1 _15il2ffl g1fwxfvr _15il2ffj\",\"id\":\"World-War-I-Planes\",\"children\":[\"World War I Planes\"],\"data-sentry-element\":\"H\",\"data-sentry-component\":\"H2\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"heading.tsx\"}]\nce:[\"$\",\"div\",\"12\",{\"className\":\"_42elsx1 o2jrjz3 g1fwxfzs g1fwxfzw\",\"data-sentry-element\":\"Component\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"box.tsx\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"World War I was the first major conflict to harness the power of planes. Though not as impactful as the British Royal Navy or Germany’s U-boats, the use of planes in World War I presaged their later, pivotal role in military conflicts around the globe.\"]}],\"data-sentry-component\":\"Box\"}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"cf:[\"$\",\"div\",\"13\",{\"className\":\"_42elsx1 o2jrjz3 g1fwxfzs g1fwxfzw\",\"data-sentry-element\":\"Component\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"box.tsx\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"At the dawn of World War I, aviation was a relatively new field; the \",[\"$\",\"$L40\",\"1\",{\"target\":\"_blank\",\"rel\":\"noopener noreferrer\",\"children\":[\"Wright brothers\"],\"data-sentry-element\":\"NextLink\",\"data-sentry-component\":\"Link\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"link.tsx\",\"href\":\"/articles/inventions/wright-brothers\",\"className\":\"mpgbmc0 _1jnz0oc1y g1fwxfm3 mpgbmc2 g1fwxflr\",\"prefetch\":false}],\" took their first sustained flight just eleven years before, in 1903. Aircraft were initially used primarily for reconnaissance missions. During the First Battle of the Marne, information passed from pilots allowed the Allies to exploit weak spots in the German lines, helping them to push Germany out of France.\"]}],\"data-sentry-component\":\"Box\"}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"d0:[\"$\",\"div\",\"14\",{\"className\":\"_42elsx1 o2jrjz3 g1fwxfzs g1fwxfzw\",\"data-sentry-element\":\"Component\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"box.tsx\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"The first machine guns were successfully mounted on planes in June of 1912 in the United States, but were imperfect; if timed incorrectly, a bullet could easily destroy the propeller of the plane it came from. The Morane-Saulnier L, a French plane, provided a solution: The propeller was armored with deflector wedges that prevented bullets from hitting it. The Morane-Saulnier Type L was used by the French, the British Royal Flying Corps (part of the Army), the British Royal Navy Air Service and the Imperial Russian Air Service. The British Bristol Type 22 was another popular model used for both reconnaissance work and as a fighter plane.\"]}],\"data-sentry-component\":\"Box\"}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"d1:[\"$\",\"div\",\"15\",{\"className\":\"_42elsx1 o2jrjz3 g1fwxfzs g1fwxfzw\",\"data-sentry-element\":\"Component\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"box.tsx\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"Dutch inventor Anthony Fokker improved upon the French deflector system in 1915. His “interrupter” synchronized the firing of the guns with the plane’s propeller to avoid collisions. Though his most popular plane during WWI was the single-seat Fokker Eindecker, Fokker created over 40 kinds of airplanes for the Germans.\"]}],\"data-sentry-component\":\"Box\"}]\nd2:[\"$\",\"div\",\"16\",{\"className\":\"_42elsx1 o2jrjz3 g1fwxfzs g1fwxfzw\",\"data-sentry-element\":\"Component\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"box.tsx\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"The Allies debuted the Handley-Page HP O/400, the first two-engine bomber, in 1915. As aerial technology progressed, long-range heavy bombers like Germany’s Gotha G.V. (first introduced in 1917) were used to strike cities like London. Their speed and maneuverability proved to be far deadlier than Germany’s earlier Zeppelin raids.\"]}],\"data-sentry-component\":\"Box\"}]\nd3:[\"$\",\"div\",\"17\",{\"className\":\"_42elsx1 o2jrjz3 g1fwxfzs g1fwxfzw\",\"data-sentry-element\":\"Component\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"box.tsx\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"By the war’s end, the Allies were producing five times more aircraft than the Germans. On April 1, 1918, the British created the Royal Air Force, or RAF, the first air force to be a separate military branch independent from the navy or army.\"]}],\"data-sentry-component\":\"Box\"}]\nd4:[\"$\",\"h2\",\"18\",{\"className\":\"_15il2ffd _15il2ffa a0xfey1 g1fwxfbr g1fwxfc1 _15il2ffl g1fwxfvr _15il2ffj\",\"id\":\"Second-Battle-of-the-Marne\",\"children\":[\"Second Battle of the Marne\"],\"data-sentry-element\":\"H\",\"data-sentry-component\":\"H2\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"heading.tsx\"}]\nd5:[\"$\",\"div\",\"19\",{\"className\":\"_42elsx1 o2jrjz3 g1fwxfzs g1fwxfzw\",\"data-sentry-element\":\"Component\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"box.tsx\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"With Germany able to build up its streng"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"th on the Western Front after the armistice with Russia, Allied troops struggled to hold off another German offensive until promised reinforcements from the United States were able to arrive.\"]}],\"data-sentry-component\":\"Box\"}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"d6:[\"$\",\"div\",\"20\",{\"className\":\"_42elsx1 o2jrjz3 g1fwxfzs g1fwxfzw\",\"data-sentry-element\":\"Component\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"box.tsx\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"On July 15, 1918, German troops launched what would become the last German offensive of the war, attacking French forces (joined by 85,000 American troops as well as some of the British Expeditionary Force) in the \",[\"$\",\"$L40\",\"1\",{\"target\":\"_blank\",\"rel\":\"noopener noreferrer\",\"children\":[\"Second Battle of the Marne\"],\"data-sentry-element\":\"NextLink\",\"data-sentry-component\":\"Link\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"link.tsx\",\"href\":\"/this-day-in-history/second-battle-of-the-marne-begins-with-final-german-offensive\",\"className\":\"mpgbmc0 _1jnz0oc1y g1fwxfm3 mpgbmc2 g1fwxflr\",\"prefetch\":false}],\". The Allies successfully pushed back the German offensive and launched their own counteroffensive just three days later.\"]}],\"data-sentry-component\":\"Box\"}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"d7:[\"$\",\"div\",\"21\",{\"className\":\"_42elsx1 o2jrjz3 g1fwxfzs g1fwxfzw\",\"data-sentry-element\":\"Component\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"box.tsx\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"After suffering massive casualties, Germany was forced to call off a planned offensive further north, in the Flanders region stretching between France and Belgium, which was envisioned as Germany’s best hope of victory.\"]}],\"data-sentry-component\":\"Box\"}]\nd8:[\"$\",\"div\",\"22\",{\"className\":\"_42elsx1 o2jrjz3 g1fwxfzs g1fwxfzw\",\"data-sentry-element\":\"Component\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"box.tsx\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"The Second Battle of the Marne turned the tide of war decisively towards the Allies, who were able to regain much of France and Belgium in the months that followed.\"]}],\"data-sentry-component\":\"Box\"}]\nd9:[\"$\",\"h2\",\"23\",{\"className\":\"_15il2ffd _15il2ffa a0xfey1 g1fwxfbr g1fwxfc1 _15il2ffl g1fwxfvr _15il2ffj\",\"id\":\"The-Harlem-Hellfighters-and-Other-All-Black-Regiments\",\"children\":[\"The Harlem Hellfighters and Other All-Black Regiments\"],\"data-sentry-element\":\"H\",\"data-sentry-component\":\"H2\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"heading.tsx\"}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"da:[\"$\",\"div\",\"24\",{\"className\":\"_42elsx1 o2jrjz3 g1fwxfzs g1fwxfzw\",\"data-sentry-element\":\"Component\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"box.tsx\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"By the time World War I began, there were four all-Black regiments in the U.S. military: the 24th and 25th Infantry and the 9th and 10th Cavalry. All four regiments comprised of celebrated soldiers who fought in the \",[\"$\",\"$L40\",\"1\",{\"target\":\"_blank\",\"rel\":\"noopener noreferrer\",\"children\":[\"Spanish-American War\"],\"data-sentry-element\":\"NextLink\",\"data-sentry-component\":\"Link\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"link.tsx\",\"href\":\"/articles/early-20th-century-us/spanish-american-war\",\"className\":\"mpgbmc0 _1jnz0oc1y g1fwxfm3 mpgbmc2 g1fwxflr\",\"prefetch\":false}],\" and \",[\"$\",\"$L40\",\"3\",{\"target\":\"_blank\",\"rel\":\"noopener noreferrer\",\"children\":[\"American-Indian Wars\"],\"data-sentry-element\":\"NextLink\",\"data-sentry-component\":\"Link\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"link.tsx\",\"href\":\"/articles/native-american-history/american-indian-wars\",\"className\":\"mpgbmc0 _1jnz0oc1y g1fwxfm3 mpgbmc2 g1fwxflr\",\"prefetch\":false}],\", and served in the American territories. But they were not deployed for overseas combat in World War I.\"]}],\"data-sentry-component\":\"Box\"}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"db:[\"$\",\"div\",null,{\"className\":\"x4or4w5 x4or4w6 g1fwxfk g1fwxfs\",\"data-sentry-element\":\"Component\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"box.tsx\",\"data-sentry-component\":\"Box\",\"children\":[\"$undefined\",[\"$\",\"$Lb4\",null,{\"mainContainerId\":\"main-content-10\",\"relatedArticles\":[],\"quotes\":[],\"adKey\":\"apluse.hist.topics\",\"adCategories\":\"$7e:props:children:0:props:adCategories\",\"articleName\":\"world-war-i-history\",\"quickFacts\":\"$undefined\",\"quickFactsLogo\":\"$94:props:children:1:props:quickFactsLogo\",\"data-sentry-element\":\"RightRail\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"rich-text-section.tsx\"}]]}]\ndd:[\"$\",\"h2\",\"3\",{\"className\":\"_15il2ffd _15il2ffa a0xfey1 g1fwxfbr g1fwxfc1 _15il2ffl g1fwxfvr _15il2ffj\",\"id\":\"World-War-I-Armistice\",\"children\":[\"World War I Armistice\"],\"data-sentry-element\":\"H\",\"data-sentry-component\":\"H2\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"heading.tsx\"}]\nde:[\"$\",\"div\",\"4\",{\"className\":\"_42elsx1 o2jrjz3 g1fwxfzs g1fwxfzw\",\"data-sentry-element\":\"Component\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"box.tsx\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"By the fall of 1918, the Central Powers were unraveling on all fronts.\"]}],\"data-sentry-component\":\"Box\"}]\ndf:[\"$\",\"div\",\"5\",{\"className\":\"_42elsx1 o2jrjz3 g1fwxfzs g1fwxfzw\",\"data-sentry-element\":\"Component\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"box.tsx\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"Despite the Turkish victory at Gallipoli, later defeats by invading forces and an Arab revolt that destroyed the Ottoman economy and devastated its land, the Turks signed a treaty with the Allies in late October 1918.\"]}],\"data-sentry-component\":\"Box\"}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"e0:[\"$\",\"div\",\"6\",{\"className\":\"_42elsx1 o2jrjz3 g1fwxfzs g1fwxfzw\",\"data-sentry-element\":\"Component\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"box.tsx\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"Austria-Hungary, dissolving from within due to growing nationalist movements among its diverse population, reached an armistice on November 4. Facing dwindling resources on the battlefield, discontent on the home front and the surrender of its allies, Germany was finally forced to seek an \",[\"$\",\"$L40\",\"1\",{\"target\":\"_blank\",\"rel\":\"noopener noreferrer\",\"children\":[\"armistice\"],\"data-sentry-element\":\"NextLink\",\"data-sentry-component\":\"Link\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"link.tsx\",\"href\":\"/this-day-in-history/november-11/world-war-i-ends\",\"className\":\"mpgbmc0 _1jnz0oc1y g1fwxfm3 mpgbmc2 g1fwxflr\",\"prefetch\":false}],\" on November 11, 1918, ending World War I.\"]}],\"data-sentry-component\":\"Box\"}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"e1:[\"$\",\"h2\",\"7\",{\"className\":\"_15il2ffd _15il2ffa a0xfey1 g1fwxfbr g1fwxfc1 _15il2ffl g1fwxfvr _15il2ffj\",\"id\":\"Treaty-of-Versailles\",\"children\":[\"Treaty of Versailles\"],\"data-sentry-element\":\"H\",\"data-sentry-component\":\"H2\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"heading.tsx\"}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"e2:[\"$\",\"div\",\"8\",{\"className\":\"_42elsx1 o2jrjz3 g1fwxfzs g1fwxfzw\",\"data-sentry-element\":\"Component\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"box.tsx\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"At the \",[\"$\",\"$L40\",\"1\",{\"target\":\"_blank\",\"rel\":\"noopener noreferrer\",\"children\":[\"Paris Peace Conference\"],\"data-sentry-element\":\"NextLink\",\"data-sentry-component\":\"Link\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"link.tsx\",\"href\":\"/this-day-in-history/post-world-war-i-peace-conference-begins-in-paris\",\"className\":\"mpgbmc0 _1jnz0oc1y g1fwxfm3 mpgbmc2 g1fwxflr\",\"prefetch\":false}],\" in 1919, Allied leaders stated their desire to build a postwar world that would safeguard itself against future conflicts of such a devastating scale.\"]}],\"data-sentry-component\":\"Box\"}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"e3:[\"$\",\"div\",\"9\",{\"className\":\"_42elsx1 o2jrjz3 g1fwxfzs g1fwxfzw\",\"data-sentry-element\":\"Component\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"box.tsx\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"Some hopeful participants had even begun calling World War I “the War to End All Wars.” But the \",[\"$\",\"$L40\",\"1\",{\"target\":\"_blank\",\"rel\":\"noopener noreferrer\",\"children\":[\"Treaty of Versailles\"],\"data-sentry-element\":\"NextLink\",\"data-sentry-component\":\"Link\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"link.tsx\",\"href\":\"/articles/world-war-i/treaty-of-versailles\",\"className\":\"mpgbmc0 _1jnz0oc1y g1fwxfm3 mpgbmc2 g1fwxflr\",\"prefetch\":false}],\", signed on June 28, 1919, would not achieve that lofty goal.\"]}],\"data-sentry-component\":\"Box\"}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"e4:[\"$\",\"div\",\"10\",{\"className\":\"_42elsx1 o2jrjz3 g1fwxfzs g1fwxfzw\",\"data-sentry-element\":\"Component\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"box.tsx\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"Saddled with war guilt, heavy reparations and denied entrance into the \",[\"$\",\"$L40\",\"1\",{\"target\":\"_blank\",\"rel\":\"noopener noreferrer\",\"children\":[\"League of Nations\"],\"data-sentry-element\":\"NextLink\",\"data-sentry-component\":\"Link\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"link.tsx\",\"href\":\"/articles/league-of-nations\",\"className\":\"mpgbmc0 _1jnz0oc1y g1fwxfm3 mpgbmc2 g1fwxflr\",\"prefetch\":false}],\", Germany felt tricked into signing the treaty, having believed any peace would be a “peace without victory,” as put forward by President Wilson in his famous \",[\"$\",\"$L40\",\"3\",{\"target\":\"_blank\",\"rel\":\"noopener noreferrer\",\"children\":[\"Fourteen Points\"],\"data-sentry-element\":\"NextLink\",\"data-sentry-component\":\"Link\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"link.tsx\",\"href\":\"/this-day-in-history/wilson-delivers-fourteen-points-speech\",\"className\":\"mpgbmc0 _1jnz0oc1y g1fwxfm3 mpgbmc2 g1fwxflr\",\"prefetch\":false}],\" speech of January 1918.\"]}],\"data-sentry-component\":\"Box\"}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"e5:[\"$\",\"div\",\"11\",{\"className\":\"_42elsx1 o2jrjz3 g1fwxfzs g1fwxfzw\",\"data-sentry-element\":\"Component\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"box.tsx\",\"children\":[\"$\",\"p\",null,{\"children\":[\"As the years passed, hatred of the Versailles treaty and its authors settled into a smoldering resentment in Germany that would, two decades later, be counted among the causes of \",[\"$\",\"$L40\",\"1\",{\"target\":\"_blank\",\"rel\":\"noopener noreferrer\",\"children\":[\"World War II\"],\"data-sentry-element\":\"NextLink\",\"data-sentry-component\":\"Link\",\"data-sentry-source-file\":\"link.tsx\",\"href\":\"/articles/world-war-ii/world-war-ii-history\",\"className\":\"mpgbmc0 _1jnz0oc1y g1fwxfm3 mpgbmc2 g1fwxflr\",\"prefetch\":false}],\".\"]}],\"data-sentry-component\":\"Box\"}]\n"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,"e6:[\"$\",\"h2\",\"12\",{\"className\":\"_15il2ffd _15il2ffa a0xfey1 g1fwxfbr g1fwxfc1 